  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p><strong>Upper Yosemite Fall</strong> rises to 6,526 feet in California. The Upper Yosemite Fall Trail covers 7.2 miles round trip (out-and-back) with about 2,700 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a steady climb. It's rated Strenuous overall.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>Most of the climbing comes early \u2014 the first half of the route does the bulk of the work before the grade eases. The steepest stretch arrives around mile 0.17, gaining roughly 4,800 feet per mile \u2014 expect a slow, deliberate pace through here. From the trailhead at about 4,000 feet, the route tops out near 6,700 feet. Along the way the route passes a viewpoint (Lower Yosemite Fall View) near mile 0.01, a viewpoint (Falls View) near mile 0.07, a rest area (Galen Clark Memorial Bench) near mile 0.07, a viewpoint (Oh My Gosh Point) near mile 0.35 \u2014 useful checkpoints for judging your pace.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=37.74272,-119.60319\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">37.74272, -119.60319</a> (tap for driving directions). It's roughly 125 miles south of Reno, Nevada. Parking: Yosemite Falls parking. Fee: $35 park entrance.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Late May through October is the main season. Snow and ice linger into spring and return early in fall at this latitude and elevation; in winter this becomes a different, more serious undertaking requiring traction devices and warm layers.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Strenuous rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 6 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from National Park Service - Yosemite.</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Lower Yosemite Fall, Panorama Trail and Mirror Lake \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
